  <data name="RichTextBox1.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Types of Data
	- Visual Basic provides numerous data types to allow programmers to optimize the use of computer memory.

A. String - Alphanumeric data such as letters of the alphabet, digits that are not treated as numbers, and other special characters.
(Memory Storage in Bytes: Size Varies)
B. Char - Stores single Unicode characters. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 2)
C. Decimal - Decimal numeric values often used to store dollars/cents. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 16)
D. Double - Double-precision numeric values with 14 digits of accuracy. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 8)
E. Single - Single-precision numeric values with 6 digits of accuracy. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 4)
F. Short - Whole numeric values in range -32, 768 to 32, 767. (Memory in Storage Bytes: 2)
G. Integer - Whole numeric values in the range -2, 147, 483, 648 to 2, 147, 483, 647. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 4)
H. Long - Whole numeric values that are very very large. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 8)
I. Boolean - True or False. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 2)
J. Byte - Stores binary data of values 0 to 255. Can be used to store ASCII character code values. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 1)
K. Date - Stores dates in the form 1/1/0001 to 12/31/9999. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 8)
L. Object - Stores data of any type. (Memory Storage in Bytes: 4)



Arithmetic Operators
	- The arithmetic operators are the same as in many other programming languages.

Addition 		(+)
Subtraction 		(-)
Multiplication 	(*)
Division 		(/)
Exponentiation 	(^)
Integer Division 	(\)
Modulus Division 	(Mod)

The order of precedence for expressions that have more than one operation is the same as for other programming languages.

First -&gt; Values enclosed inside parenthesis
	Second -&gt; Exponentiation
		Third -&gt; Multiplication and Division
			Fourth -&gt; Integer Division
				Fifth -&gt; Modulus Division
					Sixth -&gt; Addition and Subtraction</value>
